<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Surrender of Japan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2 September <a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/september-2">On this day: 2 September</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was formally signed on 2 September 1945 aboard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uss-missouri">USS Missouri</a> in Tokyo Bay. It ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> treat it as the end of the empire, followed by Allied occupation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/surrender-of-japan.jpg" alt="Surrender of Japan" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Army Signal Corps photographer LT. Stephen E. Korpanty; restored by Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamoru_Shigemitsu_signs_the_Instrument_of_Surrender,_officially_ending_the_Second_World_War.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> had suffered nearly two years of defeats in the South West Pacific, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Marianas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. After the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saipan">Saipan</a> in July 1944 General Hideki Tōjō was replaced as prime minister by General Kuniaki Koiso, and after the loss of the Philippines Koiso was replaced by Admiral Kantarō Suzuki. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> took Iwo Jima and Okinawa in the first half of 1945, and Okinawa was to be a staging area for the invasion of the Home Islands.</p><p>Policy was made by the Supreme Council for the Direction of the War, the so-called Big Six. In the summer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s leaders privately asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, then neutral, to mediate, while the Soviets prepared to attack in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in fulfilment of promises made at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalta-conference">Yalta Conferences</a>. On 26 July the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-declaration">Potsdam Declaration</a>, calling for unconditional surrender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 6 August 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> dropped an atomic bomb on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a>. Late on 8 August the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> declared war, in violation of the Soviet–Japanese Neutrality Pact, and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> soon after midnight. A second atomic bomb fell on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> on 9 August. The Big Six and the cabinet remained split, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Emperor Hirohito</a> then ordered the council to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> terms. After several more days of negotiations, including a failed coup attempt by army hardliners between 12 and 15 August, he announced the surrender in a recorded radio address on 15 August.</p><p>The Emperor gave the public the new and cruel bomb as his reason and told the military that the Soviet entry into the war threatened the empire's foundation. The occupation began on 28 August under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-commander-for-the-allied-powers">Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers</a>, and the formal ceremony took place on 2 September aboard the battleship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uss-missouri">USS Missouri</a>, where Japanese officials signed the Instrument of Surrender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Allied countries marked V-J Day. Isolated Japanese soldiers refused to surrender for months and years afterwards, some into the 1970s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> continued fighting until early September and took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuril-islands">Kuril Islands</a>, but its plan to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a> was frustrated by President Truman's opposition. The state of war formally ended when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-francisco">Treaty of San Francisco</a> came into force on 28 April 1952, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the Soviet Union ended theirs with a joint declaration in 1956. The part played by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki">atomic bombings</a> in the surrender, and the ethics of the two attacks, remain debated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Surrender of Japan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
